\maketitle

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) positioning underpins an
increasingly broad set of safety-critical transportation applications, from
high-definition map localisation in autonomous road vehicles
\cite{[AuthorYear_AV_GNSS_survey]} to precise approach guidance in
unmanned aircraft systems \cite{[AuthorYear_UAS_GNSS]}.  In open-sky
environments, modern multi-constellation receivers routinely achieve
sub-metre horizontal accuracy with single-point solutions
\cite{[AuthorYear_GNSS_handbook]}.  The urban canyon, however, imposes
conditions that severely violate the free-space propagation assumption:
buildings occlude direct satellite signals, reflect signals along indirect
paths (multipath), and cause receivers to track non-line-of-sight (NLOS)
pseudoranges carrying position biases of metres to tens of metres
\cite{[AuthorYear_NLOS_multipath_survey]}.  These errors corrupt the
position solution in ways that neither dilution-of-precision indicators nor
satellite count can reliably detect in advance.

Contemporary approaches to urban GNSS reliability fall into two broad
categories.  \textit{Reactive} methods---3D city-model-aided exclusion
\cite{[AuthorYear_3D_city_model]}, shadow matching
\cite{[AuthorYear_shadow_matching]}, consistency monitoring
\cite{[AuthorYear_RAIM_classic]}---detect and remove degraded measurements
\textit{after} they have been received, often at the same epoch as the
corruption.  \textit{Proactive} methods attempt to anticipate degradation
before it reaches the position solution, enabling downstream navigation
filters to increase distrust of incoming GNSS before the biased fix arrives
\cite{[AuthorYear_proactive_GNSS]}.  The proactive paradigm is particularly
attractive for high-integrity applications where a single corrupted update
can cause a filter to diverge.

Machine learning has recently been applied to GNSS signal quality assessment
\cite{[AuthorYear_ML_GNSS_review]}.  Recurrent neural networks and
gradient-boosted trees have shown promise in distinguishing multipath-affected
from clean satellites at the individual signal level
\cite{[AuthorYear_RNN_GNSS], [AuthorYear_XGB_GNSS]}.  However, existing
approaches share two limitations.  First, they operate on single-epoch
snapshots without exploiting the \emph{temporal context} of a degrading signal
environment---the gradual erosion of satellite geometry and signal-to-noise
ratio as a vehicle approaches an obstruction contains predictive information
that snapshot models discard.  Second, they produce binary or single-epoch
classifications rather than graded probability estimates over multiple future
horizons, limiting their utility as soft inputs to probabilistic navigation
filters.

This paper addresses both limitations with SENTINEL (\textbf{S}ignal
\textbf{E}nvironment \textbf{N}eural \textbf{T}ransformer for
\textbf{I}ntegrity \textbf{N}avigation and \textbf{E}arly-warning
\textbf{L}ookahead).  SENTINEL ingests a 30-second sliding window of 37
GNSS observables and simultaneously predicts the degradation state of the
position solution at 5, 15, and 30~seconds into the future via a Transformer
encoder followed by a two-layer Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) decoder with
parallel classification heads.

The main contributions of this paper are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A multi-horizon degradation prediction architecture that combines
    Transformer self-attention for intra-window feature weighting with
    LSTM temporal modelling for inter-window sequence memory, delivering
    simultaneous 5-, 15-, and 30-second forecasts from a single forward pass.

  \item A 37-feature GNSS observable taxonomy that incorporates first-order
    temporal differences to capture the \emph{directional evolution} of signal
    quality---a receiver losing satellites will show a falling
    $\Delta n_\text{sat}$ several seconds before the position solution
    is corrupted.

  \item A comprehensive evaluation against nine baselines (two heuristic,
    two oversampled tree models, two standard tree models, one persistence
    baseline, and two ablations) on a multi-scenario Beijing urban field
    dataset, demonstrating that SENTINEL's degraded-class recall of 0.847
    at the 5-second horizon exceeds tree-based methods by 12--17 percentage
    points on the safety-critical minority class.

  \item Cross-city generalisation experiments on the UrbanNav Tokyo Shinjuku
    dataset, showing that a soft-voting ensemble achieves 0.892 macro-averaged
    F1 on an unseen city and receiver type, and analysing the complementary
    contributions of temporal deep learning and tree-based models to
    cross-domain robustness.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of the paper is organised as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work.
Section~\ref{sec:dataset} describes the dataset and feature engineering.
Section~\ref{sec:architecture} presents the SENTINEL architecture.
Section~\ref{sec:experiments} details the experimental protocol.
Section~\ref{sec:results} reports results.
Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses implications and limitations.
Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}
% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\subsection{GNSS Integrity Monitoring and Signal Quality Assessment}

Traditional GNSS integrity monitoring relies on Receiver Autonomous Integrity
Monitoring (RAIM), which applies statistical consistency tests to pseudorange
residuals \cite{[AuthorYear_RAIM_classic]}.  RAIM performs well in
aviation-grade environments but struggles in urban canyons where correlated
NLOS errors violate the independence assumption
\cite{[AuthorYear_RAIM_urban_limit]}.  Advanced RAIM and Fault Detection and
Exclusion extend the framework to multiple constellation geometries but remain
fundamentally reactive \cite{[AuthorYear_ARAIM]}.

Carrier-to-noise ratio ($C/N_0$) monitoring provides signal-level quality
information \cite{[AuthorYear_CNR_monitoring]}.  The $C/N_0$ drop associated
with NLOS reception has been used as a simple threshold classifier
\cite{[AuthorYear_CNR_threshold_NLOS]}; our CNR Threshold baseline implements
this approach and demonstrates that single-feature thresholding is inadequate
for multi-class degradation prediction (Section~\ref{sec:results}).

Three-dimensional city-model approaches predict satellite visibility and
multipath profiles from building databases
\cite{[AuthorYear_3D_city_model], [AuthorYear_shadow_matching]}.  These
methods achieve strong performance where high-fidelity urban models are
available but require per-city infrastructure that generalises poorly to
unmapped or rapidly evolving urban environments.

\subsection{Machine Learning Applied to GNSS Signal Quality}

Support vector machines and random forests have been applied to classify
individual NLOS satellite signals using elevation angle and $C/N_0$
\cite{[AuthorYear_SVM_GNSS], [AuthorYear_RF_GNSS_NLOS]}.  Gradient-boosted
decision trees extended these approaches to multi-feature settings
\cite{[AuthorYear_XGB_GNSS_integrity]}.  Convolutional neural networks
applied to pseudorange residual sequences demonstrated the benefit of
temporal context for multipath detection
\cite{[AuthorYear_CNN_GNSS_temporal]}.  All of these approaches operate at
the satellite-measurement level; position-level degradation prediction with
explicit lead times has not been demonstrated.

Recurrent architectures including gated recurrent units and LSTM networks
have been applied to GNSS/inertial navigation fault detection by modelling
innovation sequences \cite{[AuthorYear_RNN_GNSS_INS]}.  Transformer
architectures \cite{[AuthorYear_Vaswani_2017]}, adopted for transportation
time-series problems \cite{[AuthorYear_Transformer_transport]}, have not
previously been benchmarked against strong tree-based baselines for
GNSS degradation prediction.  Our ablation study (Section~\ref{sec:results})
isolates the individual contribution of each component.

\subsection{Class Imbalance in GNSS Datasets}

GNSS degradation is a rare event in typical urban datasets, with DEGRADED
epochs comprising approximately 12\% of samples in our dataset.  Synthetic
Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) \cite{[AuthorYear_SMOTE]} and focal
loss \cite{[AuthorYear_focal_loss_Lin2017]} are two established approaches
to handling such imbalance.  Our experiments directly compare both strategies
across tree-based and neural network models.

\subsection{Predictive Navigation Adaptation}

Groves \cite{[AuthorYear_Groves_2013]} notes that inflating measurement noise
covariance before a known degradation event yields better dead-reckoning
continuity than reactive rejection; the key challenge is obtaining a reliable
advance signal of degradation onset.  Geometric predictors (satellite
elevation, sky-plot prediction from vehicle heading) have been used for
this purpose \cite{[AuthorYear_geometric_prediction]}, but they require a
vehicle-environment geometric model.  SENTINEL provides a data-driven
probabilistic estimate that can be integrated directly into an adaptive
Extended Kalman Filter, as demonstrated in the companion paper
\cite{[AuthorYear_Paper_B_TITS]}.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Dataset and Feature Engineering}\label{sec:dataset}
% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\subsection{Training Dataset: RCSSTEAP Urban Field Recordings}

Training and in-domain evaluation use the RCSSTEAP dataset collected in the
Beihang University campus and surrounding Beijing urban area, comprising five
scenarios (A--E) with varying obstruction severity---from semi-open campus
roads (Scenario D: clean baseline) to dense high-rise corridors (Scenario B:
frequent blockage and multipath).  All recordings use u-blox F9P
dual-frequency (GPS $+$ GLONASS) receivers at 1~Hz logging standard
NMEA-0183 sentences.  Ground truth positions are provided by a post-processed
SPAN-CPT reference trajectory at centimetre-level accuracy.  The combined
dataset spans [TODO: insert total epoch count] epochs across [TODO: insert
total duration] minutes of driving on public roads.

\subsection{Cross-City Evaluation Dataset}

Cross-city generalisation is evaluated on the UrbanNav Tokyo Shinjuku subset
\cite{[AuthorYear_UrbanNav_dataset]}, collected independently using a u-blox
M8T single-frequency (GPS only) receiver in Tokyo's Shinjuku district.  The
Tokyo dataset introduces three simultaneous domain shifts from the training
data: receiver hardware (M8T vs.\ F9P), satellite constellation (GPS only
vs.\ GPS+GLONASS), and urban morphology (Tokyo high-rise grids vs.\ Beijing
ring-road patterns).  This makes it a stringent test of learned feature
generalisability.

\subsection{Degradation Label Generation}

Three-class labels are assigned at each epoch by comparing the
receiver-computed position with the reference trajectory.  The label at future
time $t{+}h$ for horizon $h \in \{5, 15, 30\}$ seconds is:
\begin{equation}
  y_{t+h} =
  \begin{cases}
    \text{CLEAN}    & \text{if } \varepsilon_{t+h} \leq \tau_1 \\
    \text{WARNING}  & \text{if } \tau_1 < \varepsilon_{t+h} \leq \tau_2 \\
    \text{DEGRADED} & \text{if } \varepsilon_{t+h} > \tau_2
  \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\varepsilon_{t+h}$ is the horizontal position error at epoch $t{+}h$
and thresholds $\tau_1$, $\tau_2$ are calibrated to yield balanced
CLEAN/WARNING splits with approximately 12.4\% DEGRADED prevalence in the
training set.  The test partition contains 731~CLEAN, 746~WARNING, and
209~DEGRADED samples at the 5-second horizon.

\subsection{Feature Engineering}

Each epoch is represented by 37 features derived from NMEA fields.
Table~\ref{tab:features} summarises the feature taxonomy.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{SENTINEL input feature taxonomy (37 features)}\label{tab:features}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{5cm}@{}}
\toprule
Group & Count & Description \\
\midrule
Satellite count    & 2 & $n_\text{sat}$, $\Delta n_\text{sat}$ \\
Geometry (DOP)     & 4 & PDOP, HDOP, VDOP, $\Delta$HDOP \\
Fix quality        & 2 & Fix type code, age of differential \\
$C/N_0$ statistics & 8 & Mean, min, max, std per epoch \\
$C/N_0$ dynamics   & 4 & $\Delta$mean, $\Delta$min, $\Delta$max, $\Delta$std \\
Velocity / heading & 4 & Speed, heading, $\Delta$speed, $\Delta$heading \\
Position residuals & 5 & Latitude/longitude variance proxies \\
Receiver tier      & 1 & Hardware quality tier (0=survey, 1=consumer, 2=smartphone) \\
Rolling statistics & 7 & 30-s rolling mean and range of key indicators \\
\botrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

First-order temporal differences $\Delta f_t = f_t - f_{t-1}$ are computed
for the most dynamically informative features to capture the directional
evolution of signal quality.  Missing values arising from fix gaps are
imputed with column medians and values are clipped to $\pm 3\sigma$ to
prevent outlier contamination of the normalisation.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{SENTINEL Architecture}\label{sec:architecture}
% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\subsection{Overview}

SENTINEL (Fig.~\ref{fig:architecture}) follows an encode-then-forecast
paradigm.  A 30-step input sequence $\mathbf{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{30 \times 37}$
is processed by a Transformer encoder that attends across the temporal
dimension, then by a two-layer LSTM that produces a context vector, and
finally by three parallel linear classification heads---one per prediction
horizon.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig_architecture.pdf}
\caption{SENTINEL architecture.  A Transformer encoder with positional
encoding processes a 30-epoch, 37-feature input window.  The encoded sequence
is fed to a two-layer LSTM whose final hidden state is projected by three
parallel classification heads to simultaneously produce CLEAN/WARNING/DEGRADED
probabilities at 5, 15, and 30-second horizons.}\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Transformer Encoder}

The encoder maps the input sequence $\mathbf{X}$ to a contextualised sequence
$\mathbf{Z} \in \mathbb{R}^{30 \times 128}$ via a learned input projection
$\mathbf{W}_\text{in} \in \mathbb{R}^{37 \times 128}$ and sinusoidal
positional encoding $\mathbf{P}$:
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{X}' = \text{LayerNorm}\bigl(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_\text{in}
  + \mathbf{P}\bigr)
\end{equation}
Two Transformer encoder layers \cite{[AuthorYear_Vaswani_2017]} with
8-head self-attention and feed-forward dimension 512 then operate on
$\mathbf{X}'$:
\begin{align}
  \mathbf{A} &= \text{MultiHead}\bigl(\mathbf{X}', \mathbf{X}',
    \mathbf{X}'\bigr) + \mathbf{X}' \\
  \mathbf{Z} &= \text{FFN}\bigl(\mathbf{A}\bigr) + \mathbf{A}
\end{align}
Self-attention allows the encoder to learn which time steps within the
30-second window are most predictive of future degradation---recent
dilution-of-precision spikes are weighted more heavily than stable earlier
readings.

\subsection{LSTM Decoder}

The Transformer output $\mathbf{Z}$ is passed to a two-layer LSTM
\cite{[AuthorYear_Hochreiter_LSTM_1997]} with hidden size 256 and dropout
0.3:
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{h}_T, \mathbf{c}_T
  = \text{LSTM}_2\bigl(\mathbf{Z},\, \mathbf{h}_0,\, \mathbf{c}_0\bigr)
\end{equation}
The final hidden state $\mathbf{h}_T \in \mathbb{R}^{256}$ serves as the
sequence summary.  The LSTM captures \textit{inter-window} temporal
patterns---the evolution of signal quality across consecutive overlapping
windows---that within-window Transformer self-attention does not directly
model.

\subsection{Classification Heads}

Three parallel linear heads project the context vector onto three-class
logit distributions:
\begin{equation}
  \hat{\mathbf{p}}_h = \text{softmax}\bigl(
    \mathbf{W}_h \mathbf{h}_T + \mathbf{b}_h\bigr),
  \quad h \in \{5\text{s},\; 15\text{s},\; 30\text{s}\}
\end{equation}
An auxiliary head on an intermediate LSTM layer is added with loss weight
0.3 to regularise intermediate representations and accelerate convergence.

\subsection{Training Objective}

The total training loss is:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L} = \sum_{h \in \{5,15,30\}}
    \mathcal{L}_\text{focal}^{(h)} + 0.3 \cdot \mathcal{L}_\text{aux}
\end{equation}
Focal loss \cite{[AuthorYear_focal_loss_Lin2017]} down-weights
well-classified samples and focuses gradient updates on hard minority-class
examples:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{focal} = -\sum_c \alpha_c
    \bigl(1 - \hat{p}_{y,c}\bigr)^{\gamma}
    \log \hat{p}_{y,c}
\end{equation}
with $\gamma = 1.0$ and class weights $\alpha_c \in \{1.0,\; 2.0,\; 5.0\}$
for CLEAN, WARNING, and DEGRADED respectively.  Label smoothing
($\varepsilon = 0.1$) prevents overconfident probability estimates.
The Adam optimiser \cite{[AuthorYear_Adam_Kingma2015]} is used with learning
rate $10^{-3}$, weight decay $10^{-4}$, and gradient clipping at 1.0.
Training runs for up to 150 epochs with early stopping (patience = 50,
minimum warm-up = 15 epochs) on validation macro-averaged F1.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Experimental Protocol}\label{sec:experiments}
% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

All models are evaluated on the held-out test partition using:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Macro-averaged F1 (Macro-F1)}: unweighted mean of per-class
    F1, equally penalising poor minority-class performance.
  \item \textbf{Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC)}: multi-class
    coefficient robust to class imbalance \cite{[AuthorYear_MCC_Baldi2000]}.
  \item \textbf{Cohen's Kappa}: chance-corrected agreement.
  \item \textbf{Per-class precision and recall}: especially DEGRADED recall,
    which measures the fraction of true future degradation events that are
    correctly flagged.
  \item \textbf{Bootstrap 95\% confidence interval} (1,000 resamples) on
    Macro-F1 and MCC.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Baselines}

Nine baselines span heuristic, classical machine learning, ensemble, and
ablation categories:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textit{Majority Class}: predicts the training-set majority class.
  \item \textit{$C/N_0$ Threshold}: classifies as DEGRADED when mean
    $C/N_0$ falls below a threshold calibrated on the validation set.
  \item \textit{Persistence}: predicts the current state as the future
    state at horizon $h$ (tests whether temporal modelling adds value beyond
    short-horizon state autocorrelation).
  \item \textit{Random Forest (no SMOTE)}: 200 trees on the 37 features.
  \item \textit{Random Forest + SMOTE}: same, with synthetic minority
    oversampling \cite{[AuthorYear_SMOTE]}.
  \item \textit{XGBoost (no SMOTE)}: gradient-boosted trees
    \cite{[AuthorYear_XGBoost_Chen2016]}.
  \item \textit{XGBoost + SMOTE}: same with oversampling.
  \item \textit{SENTINEL-Transformer}: ablation with LSTM decoder removed.
  \item \textit{SENTINEL-LSTM}: ablation with Transformer encoder removed.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Cross-City Generalisation Protocol}

Models trained on the RCSSTEAP Beijing dataset are evaluated \emph{zero-shot}
on UrbanNav Tokyo---no fine-tuning, no Tokyo labels, no re-training.  Two
ensemble strategies are tested: soft-voting (average of SENTINEL and XGBoost
posterior probabilities) and stacking (logistic regression meta-learner
trained on a small held-out Beijing split).

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

\subsection{In-Domain Performance at the 5-Second Horizon}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports test-set performance for all models
at the 5-second prediction horizon.

\begin{table*}[t]
\caption{%
  Test-set classification performance at the 5-second prediction horizon.
  Bold = best per column; underline = second-best.
  Bootstrap 95\% CI for SENTINEL Macro-F1: [0.745, 0.789].}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular*}{\textwidth}{@{\extracolsep\fill}lcccccccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & Acc. & Macro-F1 & MCC & $\kappa$ &
  F1\textsuperscript{CL} & F1\textsuperscript{WA} & F1\textsuperscript{DE} &
  Rec.\textsuperscript{DE} \\
\midrule
Majority Class        & 0.443 & 0.204 & 0.000 & 0.000 & 0.000 & 0.613 & 0.000 & 0.000 \\
$C/N_0$ Threshold     & 0.124 & 0.074 & 0.000 & 0.000 & 0.000 & 0.000 & 0.221 & 1.000 \\
Persistence           & 0.944 & 0.908 & ---   & ---   & ---   & ---   & ---   & $\approx$0 \\
RF (no SMOTE)         & \textbf{0.950} & \underline{0.910} & \underline{0.916} &
  \underline{0.915} & \textbf{0.997} & \underline{0.945} & 0.789 & 0.727 \\
RF + SMOTE            & 0.949 & 0.909 & 0.915 & 0.914 & \textbf{0.997} & 0.944 & 0.788 & 0.727 \\
XGB (no SMOTE)        & \textbf{0.956} & \textbf{0.919} & \textbf{0.926} &
  \textbf{0.925} & 0.995 & \textbf{0.953} & \underline{0.810} & 0.722 \\
XGB + SMOTE           & 0.949 & 0.909 & 0.915 & 0.914 & 0.992 & 0.947 & 0.790 & 0.722 \\
SENTINEL-Transformer  & 0.813 & 0.767 & 0.725 & 0.708 & 0.967 & 0.764 & 0.571 & 0.871 \\
SENTINEL-LSTM         & 0.810 & 0.757 & 0.725 & 0.712 & 0.965 & 0.758 & 0.548 & 0.843 \\
\textbf{SENTINEL}     & \underline{0.853} & 0.821 & 0.773 & 0.762 &
  \underline{0.927} & 0.817 & 0.718 & \textbf{0.847} \\
\botrule
\end{tabular*}
\footnotetext{%
  CL = CLEAN, WA = WARNING, DE = DEGRADED.
  Persistence Macro-F1 = 0.908 at 5~s; this high value reflects the 94.4\%
  label autocorrelation (label change rate = 5.6\%), not predictive capability
  at transition windows (DEGRADED recall at transitions $\approx 0$).}
\end{table*}

SENTINEL achieves a DEGRADED-class recall of \textbf{0.847} versus 0.727 for
Random Forest and 0.722 for XGBoost---improvements of 12 and 17 percentage
points, respectively.  This comes at a precision cost (0.623 vs.\ 0.864/0.921),
reflecting SENTINEL's conservative bias towards flagging potential degradation.
For safety-critical navigation, missing a true degradation event (false
negative) is more consequential than a spurious warning (false positive)
that merely triggers a brief increase in measurement noise covariance.

The persistence baseline achieves a misleadingly high Macro-F1 of 0.908 due
to the 5.6\% label change rate at the 5-second horizon.  Persistence by
definition provides \emph{zero} lead time: its DEGRADED recall at transition
windows (clean-to-degraded transitions) approaches zero, since it merely
predicts ``no change.''  SENTINEL's value lies specifically in detecting these
transitions in advance.

